 [{:title
   "Law-suite judged ops are a hand-maintained parallel list — a future registered write op silently escapes the harness (the exact drift item 3 exists to kill)",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj",
   :detail
   "observations-laws defaults judged-ops to (keys op-fixtures) (line 339), and both drivers (observations_test.clj:36, observations_mongo_test.clj:75) assert per-op laws over (keys laws/op-fixtures). Nothing anywhere asserts (set (keys op-fixtures)) = (operations/registered-write-operations). So when someone adds :record-new-kind! to the port schema + registry, every existing guard stays green — the registry/port completeness suite in operations_test.clj is derived, the 017B wrapper eager check passes because the op IS registered — yet the law harness silently never judges the new record kind. That is precisely the 'partial coverage mistaken for full port-contract coverage' failure mode the card's item 3 was written to eliminate; item 2 got exactly this kind of completeness guard (unclassified-port-operations) but item 3's own oracle was left hand-synced. One-line fix: a test asserting fixture keys equal (operations/registered-write-operations). Not a live bug today — all 7 current write ops are covered and both adapters pass — but it is a latent coverage hole in the delivered mechanism.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017n",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Verified against the actual code: the claim is factually accurate. observations_laws.clj:339 defaults judged-ops to (keys op-fixtures), both drivers (observations_test.clj:36, observations_mongo_test.clj:75) iterate (keys laws/op-fixtures), and operations_test.clj — despite having five registry/port completeness tests (every-port-write-is-registered, every-registered-operation-is-a-port-write, no-port-operation-is-unclassified, etc.) — contains no assertion that op-fixtures keys equal (operations/registered-write-operations). A repo-wide grep confirms no such fixture-completeness check exists anywhere in test/. Scope-wise this is squarely on-card: ENG-017N's Intent states the bundle exists 'so the assurance kernel's guarantees are live and drift-proof, not merely provable,' and item 3's stated rationale is 'so partial coverage isn't mistaken for full port-contract coverage.' Item 2 — the identical drift class (a name-based oracle that a future op silently escapes) — received exactly this completeness guard (unclassified-port-operations + guard test), while item 3's own newly created hand-maintained list was left unguarded. That asymmetry is in the card's own delivered diff, not grandfathered code. It is not documented anywhere as an accepted tradeoff (card comments cover items 1-3 and deferrals but never mention fixture/registry drift). The counterargument that fixtures can't be auto-derived does not refute the finding — item 2's classifier can't be auto-derived either, yet it got a guard test; a one-line set-equality test makes the inevitable manual step loud instead of silent. Admittedly not a live bug (all 7 current ops covered, both adapters green), but this card exists precisely to eliminate latent not-yet-live coverage holes — that is its entire purpose, inherited from the 017A/B/D re-reviews — so a demonstrable, cheap-to-close hole in the delivered drift-proofing mechanism is promotion-gating for this specific card."
    "Verified the claim against the code: observations_laws.clj:339 defaults judged-ops to (keys op-fixtures), both drivers (observations_test.clj:36, observations_mongo_test.clj:75) iterate (keys laws/op-fixtures), and rg across src/ and test/ confirms nothing asserts (set (keys op-fixtures)) = (operations/registered-write-operations). operations_test.clj's completeness suite only ties port-write-operations to the registry — the fixture table is a third, hand-maintained list with no completeness oracle. The failure scenario is real: adding :record-new-kind! to the port schema + registry keeps every existing test green (classifier passes, registry complete, wrapper eager check passes) while the law harness silently never judges the new op. Refutation on grounds of 'latent, not a live bug' fails because this card's own Intent section states all three items are latent gaps by design — the card exists to make guarantees 'live and drift-proof, not merely provable.' Item 3's stated purpose is literally 'so partial coverage isn't mistaken for full port-contract coverage,' and item 2 received exactly this class of guard (unclassified-port-operations + teeth test), establishing the card's own precedent that the oracle must not be hand-synced. The card's IMPLEMENTED comment documents no accepted tradeoff leaving the fixture list unguarded. The fix is a one-line test, it is squarely in the card's delivered scope, and it is the same drift class item 2 was required to kill — a coherent, in-scope, should-fix finding."]}
