  {:title
   "Mongo connection leaks if profile/resolve-adapters throws during composition in make-status-adapters / run-serve :services",
   :file "src/epiphany/infra/main.clj",
   :detail
   "make-status-adapters :services (lines 186-198) and run-serve :services (lines 652-663) connect Mongo, then call profile/resolve-adapters OUTSIDE any try/finally — unlike run-register (114-123), make-ingest-adapters (469-476), and with-observations-adapter (811-817), which all compose inside try/finally (mongo/disconnect! conn). If composition throws after connect (e.g. Files/createDirectories on ~/.epiphany/index fails — permissions, disk), the connection is never disconnected. Impact is process-lifetime only (the exception propagates to an exit-1 result), so this is cleanup-discipline asymmetry rather than a live leak, but it is exactly the class the retarget should have made uniform.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017n",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The code facts are accurate (make-status-adapters :services at main.clj:186-198 and run-serve :services at 652-663 do compose outside try/finally, unlike run-register/make-ingest-adapters/with-observations-adapter), but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating. (1) Self-acknowledged nit with no live leak: every failure path in these two sites propagates to an {:exit 1} result in a short-lived CLI process (run-status) or a serve process that exits on composition failure; the OS reclaims the socket at process teardown. There is no long-lived process in which the connection silently persists. (2) The lifecycle split is deliberate and documented: ENG-017N's implementation comment on the card records that profile/resolve-raw-adapters 'requires explicit :mongo-conn (lifecycle stays with caller)', and make-status-adapters' docstring contracts that it returns a :cleanup fn the caller owns — the try/finally sites are ones where the helper itself invokes f, the map-returning sites delegate cleanup to the caller. (3) Out of delivered scope: the card's item 1 acceptance criterion is that CLI paths build their port through the validating wrapper (proven by the with-redefs spy tests), not that connection-cleanup discipline be uniform across all call sites; nothing in scope, non-goals, or acceptance criteria names connection lifecycle. A consistency nicety worth a future chore, not a gate on review→done."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the code shape (make-status-adapters lines 186-198 and run-serve lines 652-663 connect Mongo then compose via profile/resolve-adapters outside try/finally; lucene/make-index-adapter does call Files/createDirectories at construction, so composition can throw after connect). But it fails every gate for blocking promotion: (1) The claim itself concedes the impact is 'process-lifetime only' and self-labels severity 'nit' — on a composition throw both commands return {:exit 1} and the CLI process exits, so the Mongo client dies with the JVM; there is no live leak. (2) The connection-lifecycle split is deliberate and documented: profile.clj's resolve-raw-adapters docstring states ':mongo-conn (connection lifecycle stays with the caller)', a design decision this card introduced explicitly. (3) The run-serve pattern is grandfathered — git show 06cd710^ shows run-serve already connected Mongo and composed adapters (via make-durable-index-ports, same directory-creating I/O) with no try/finally before this card; the card retargeted composition onto resolve-adapters but did not introduce the connect-then-compose-without-finally structure. (4) Out of delivered scope: the card's acceptance criteria are the validating wrapper being live on CLI paths (proven by spy tests), the classifier guard, and law-harness generalization — connection-cleanup uniformity across call sites is not an acceptance criterion, and the card's completion comment records all gates green (712 unit tests, boundary-check clean). A nit-level, process-lifetime-only cleanup asymmetry in grandfathered structure does not gate a review→done promotion."]}
