  {:title
   "Generalized idempotency laws are weaker than the laws they replaced — stored-fact-unchanged and conflict-payload assertions silently dropped",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/observations_laws.clj",
   :detail
   "Pre-06cd710, law-idempotent-replay-stable verified BOTH that an identical replay returns nil AND that the stored fact is unchanged (`(not= record ((:find-by-request-id port) rid))`), and law-changed-content-replay-conflicts verified the conflict code AND that the result carries the offending :request-id AND that the stored fact remains the original. The rewritten laws (lines 229-265) check only the return value: law-idempotent-replay-stable now only asserts replay-returns-nil; law-changed-content-replay's :full branch only checks (:code result), and its :first-write-wins branch only checks nil. `find-by-request-id` no longer appears anywhere in the file. This contradicts the harness's own docstring (lines 18-19: ':first-write-wins — any replay returns nil; the stored fact stays the original') and the card's completion comment ('first-write-wins ... original retained — matches BOTH adapters' actual semantics, verified differentially') — 'original retained' is not tested by any law or differential check. An adapter that silently overwrites on replay would now pass the full suite. For a card whose stated purpose is hardening the assurance kernel, weakening proven laws while claiming the stronger guarantee in docs is exactly the false-green failure mode ENG-017C/D were built to retire.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017n",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim's code reading is accurate — the generalized laws at lines 229-265 check only return values and `find-by-request-id` no longer appears in the law file, whereas pre-06cd710 the laws also asserted stored-fact-unchanged and conflict :request-id. But the claimed consequence is wrong for everything in scope. The dropped assertions were NOT silently lost: they survive as explicit adapter-level tests for both shipped adapters — in_memory_test.clj `idempotent-replay-stable` (line 130: 'Stored fact is unchanged') and `changed-content-replay-conflicts` (lines 144-146: conflict carries :request-id AND stored fact must be the original), and mongo_test.clj `idempotency-conflict-returns-shared-category` (lines 105-108, same assertions against the real Mongo adapter). Repository-location is the only `:full`-idempotency op, so the exact behavior the reviewer says is untested is tested on every adapter that can exhibit it. For the `:first-write-wins` ops (review-decision, lineage-candidate) there was ZERO law coverage before this card — the pre-06cd710 harness only fixed repository-location — so those kinds strictly gained coverage (replay-returns-nil laws plus existing adapter tests like review-decision-idempotent-by-request-id asserting count==1 after retry). The 'silently overwriting adapter passes' scenario applies only to a hypothetical future third adapter, not to anything this card ships. The one real residue is a docstring nit (ns doc lines 18-19 still say 'stored fact stays the original' for a guarantee the law itself no longer checks directly) — a wording fix, not a false-green on shipped behavior. Net effect of the card: law coverage expanded from 1 op to 7 with no shipped guarantee losing its test. Not promotion-gating."
    "Verified against git history: pre-06cd710 (6a3debe), law-idempotent-replay-stable asserted `(not= record ((:find-by-request-id port) rid))` ('stored fact changed after an identical replay') and law-changed-content-replay-conflicts asserted the conflict carries `:request-id` AND the stored fact remains the original. Commit 06cd710 (this card) rewrote both laws to check only return values; `find-by-request-id` no longer appears in observations_laws.clj. The claim is factually exact. Not a documented tradeoff: the completion comment does not disclose the dropped assertions — it overclaims ('first-write-wins ... original retained — matches BOTH adapters' actual semantics, verified differentially'), and the harness docstring (lines 16-20) still states 'the stored fact stays the original' for both :full and :first-write-wins kinds, a guarantee no law now tests. Not covered elsewhere: adapter-level tests verify stored-fact-original only for repository-location (in_memory_test.clj:130/146, mongo_test.clj:107); for the :first-write-wins ops (review-decision, lineage-candidate) only identical-replay-returns-nil is tested — a conflicting replay that silently overwrites passes everything. In scope: item 3 of the card is exactly this file, and the card's own Intent frames its mission as eliminating 'a proven guarantee [that] is not actually exercised on the paths that matter' — this change creates precisely that gap in the judgment harness while claiming the guarantee in docs. Restoration is cheap (law-rejection-leaves-state-unchanged already demonstrates the export-all snapshot technique, which works for all ops regardless of request-id keying). Real, in-scope, undocumented, and central to a hardening card's acceptance — survives the skeptic pass."]}
