  {:title
   "Classifier guard cannot catch a write op named with a read-class prefix",
   :file "src/epiphany/law/operations.clj",
   :detail
   "unclassified-port-operations (lines 177-187) only catches ops matching NO class. A future durable write named :list-archived! or :find-and-store! would classify as 'read' via read-operation? (lines 166-175), escaping BOTH the 017B wrapper's eager unregistered-op check (which filters on write-operation?) and the new guard (it is classified — wrongly). The card's stated example (:append!) is caught and the test classifier-guard-has-teeth proves it; the residual wrong-class-collision gap is inherent to name-based classification and the ns comment is honest about the mechanism, but the guard does not fully deliver 'a non-conforming write-op name cannot silently escape' for prefix-collision names.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017n",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The mechanism described is real but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating on three grounds. (1) Self-declared nit severity: the reviewer themselves labels this a nit, and the skeptic contract is that nits do not gate a review→done promotion. (2) In-scope delivery matches the card's ask: Scope item 2 names exactly the failure mode the guard must catch — 'a future durable write named otherwise (:append!, :save-observation!)' matching NO convention, slipping past the shared oracle. The delivered guard (unclassified-port-operations + the teeth test) closes precisely that, and the acceptance criterion 'a non-conforming write-op name cannot silently escape registration/validation' is demonstrated. The residual case the reviewer raises is a different, narrower hypothetical: a future op whose name is semantically contradictory (a durable write deliberately named with a read prefix like :list-archived!), which would misclassify rather than escape classification. (3) The limitation is documented, not hidden: the ns/comment block at lines 176-183 openly states 'write-operation? classifies BY NAME' and frames the guard as catching ops outside every class; destructive-port-operations (explicit, hand-reviewed set) shows the established pattern for handling names that defy the convention — the codebase already has a documented mechanism (explicit listing) for exactly this kind of exception. Nothing is silently wrong today: the current port schema contains no such prefix-collision op, so this is a speculative future-naming hazard that the card never promised to solve, honestly bounded in the code comments, and explicitly conceded as a nit by its own author."
    "The mechanism described is technically accurate (read-operation? at operations.clj:191-200 matches :find-*/:list-* prefixes, so a hypothetical future write named :list-archived! would classify as read and pass unclassified-port-operations, and the 017B wrapper's eager check at application/validation.clj:66 does filter on write-operation?), but the finding does not survive as promotion-gating. (1) Scope: the card's item 2 and acceptance criterion target a write op named outside ALL conventions — the stated examples are :append! and :save-observation! — and that exact gap is delivered and tested (classifier-guard-has-teeth at operations_test.clj:34 proves :append! classifies as nothing and fails the guard). The wrong-class prefix-collision scenario is a different, hypothetical future-mistake gap the card never promised to close. (2) Documented tradeoff: the ns-level comment at operations.clj:176-183 explicitly states the classifier works BY NAME and frames the guard as 'anything else is a visible failure' — the residual limitation of name-based classification is disclosed in the delivered code, not hidden. (3) Severity: the reviewer self-rated this a nit about a scenario requiring a future author to name a durable write with a read-class prefix AND a bang, which no current port entry does; the destructive class (#{:clear-all!}) is explicitly hand-reviewed for exactly this reason. A nit-grade, documented, out-of-delivered-scope residual does not gate review→done."]}
